<Layout title="FM-1 Pocket Player — suprlab"><div class="wrap"><header class="article-head"><a class="eyebrow" href={`${base}#projects`}>SUPRLAB / SELECTED PROJECTS</a><h1>FM-1 Pocket Player</h1><p>A small box. A lot of sound. Explore M-VAVE FM-1 community firmware in your browser.</p><div class="project-links"><a href="https://slowphase.github.io/fm1-pocket-player/">Play in your browser</a><a href="https://github.com/slowphase/fm1-pocket-player">Repository</a><a href={`${base}guides/`}>Explore the guides</a></div></header><img class="article-image" src={`${base}projects/fm1-player.png`} alt="FM-1 Pocket Player with its firmware library open"/><article class="prose project-prose"><h2>Get curious. Turn a knob.</h2><p>Choose a firmware and its screen, sound, keys and knobs come to life on the page. No hardware or installation needed. Use your mouse, touch screen or computer keyboard to explore.</p><h2>A whole library of possibilities</h2><ul><li>A searchable firmware library, with screenshots and favourites.</li><li>A playable FM-1 panel, with keys, knobs, buttons and lights.</li><li>Sound and MIDI input in browsers that support Web MIDI.</li><li>Support for your own .rfm1 files.</li><li>Favourites and settings saved in your browser.</li></ul><p>The firmware runs through WebAssembly: its own code, rebuilt for the browser. Pocket Player plays .rfm1 files, rather than the .fwsc packages installed on a physical FM-1.</p><h2>A community effort</h2><p>Built on <a href="https://github.com/jakehandy/fm1-browser-simulator">Jake Handy’s FM-1 Browser Simulator</a>, with a firmware library sourced from <a href="https://github.com/Quixotic7/RemoteFM1FW">RemoteFM1FW, maintained by Michael P Jones (Quixotic7)</a>. Each firmware remains the work of its respective authors.</p><p>FM-1 Pocket Player is an independent community project and is not affiliated with M-VAVE.</p></article></div></Layout>
